<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	DRAFT — needs human review before submission. See STATUS.md Phase 5 for the full
	reasoning. In short:

	- NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes: @capacitor/preferences wraps NSUserDefaults on iOS, which is
	  one of Apple's "required reason" API categories. The reason code below (CA92.1) is our
	  best-guess match for "storing information generated/tracked on-device and never leaving
	  the device" — Apple's approved reason list has several UserDefaults reason codes with
	  subtly different meanings, and only whoever ships this build can confirm which one
	  actually matches how @capacitor/preferences is used at runtime (it also backs the
	  Supabase auth session store on native — see src/lib/supabase/client.ts — so double-check
	  the "does this data leave the device" framing still holds for that use case too).
	- No other installed Capacitor plugin (@capacitor/app, @capacitor/core, @capacitor/ios,
	  @capacitor/splash-screen, @capacitor/status-bar) was identified as wrapping a
	  required-reason API based on what each plugin does, but this file does not include a
	  review of Capacitor's own internal/native runtime code or of Apple's exact required-reason
	  API list against every symbol actually linked into the binary — that level of certainty
	  needs Xcode's own build-time analysis (or Apple's static analysis on App Store Connect
	  upload, which will flag anything missing).
	- NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes reflects what this session's code audit found actually being
	  collected (see STATUS.md Phase 3/6): account email (for Supabase Auth) and the user's own
	  workout/training data, synced to the developer's own Supabase backend for the app's own
	  functionality, never shared with third parties or used for tracking/advertising. This
	  must match whatever is declared in App Store Connect's separate "App Privacy" questionnaire
	  — that questionnaire is filled out by a human in App Store Connect and is not generated by
	  this file, but the two should agree.
	- This file also has NOT been added to the Xcode project/target yet (adding a file on disk
	  does not put it in project.pbxproj's Copy Bundle Resources build phase) — that has to be
	  done once in Xcode: drag PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy into the App target's file list, or an
	  ordinary "commit and open in Xcode" step will surface it as an untracked file at worst.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeFitnessAndHealth</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
